g problem in  deep mind's alpha fold  and introduce the exciting emerging  field of ai  driven molecular design so if you're  eager to set your first steps into one  of the most promising areas of modern  science  i hope you're ready to dive in deep my  name is xander and welcome back  to archive insights  so what are proteins and why should i  care  well the very first thing that i'd like  you to contemplate is that  you and i in fact every living  biological entity on this planet  is in essence an incredibly complicated  and  interconnected molecular machine  and what i mean by this is that every  process  inside our bodies no matter how  complicated or how  magical it may seem they're all governed  by the same laws of nature  that we study in fields like chemistry  and elementary physics  and now the reason that these simple  natural laws give rise to the enormous  complexity of life  is that over billions of years of  evolution the tiny atomic particles that  make up our universe  well they started coalescing into ever  more complex formations that gradually  diversified  and started interacting with one another  now there are many  open questions in this field of  evolutionary chemistry or abiogenesis as  it's called  but what we do know is that at some  point in the evolution of life  simple organic molecules started forming  small  self-replicating systems which then  became subject to random mutations in  natural selection  and over billions of years these systems  gradually diversified and produced  more familiar biological elements like  cell barriers  genetic material and the ability to  capture ever more energy from their  environment  through processes like photosynthesis  for example  now to get an even richer appreciation  of this miracle of life  i think it's useful to get a sense of  scale with respect to the concept that  we'll be talking about in these videos  so if we start out with a small object a  familiar size let's say a coffee bean or  a grain of rice  that measures several millimeters across  and we gradually zoom in  well we soon encounter things like  amoeba which are  fairly large but unicellular organisms  going deeper we encounter things like  the human egg cell and sperm cell  measuring  several micrometers across next let's  dive  inside these cells and look at what  they're made of here we find things like  the chromosome which is  essentially a huge storage container for  our dna inside the nucleus  of every living cell next we find things  like  bacteria for example here the  mitochondria which are like  the power plants inside of our cells  they burn sugar molecules  to produce atp which is the main unit of  chemical energy  that is used to power all other  processes going on  in the cell's interior and if we zoom in  even more we have to start measuring  things in nanometers  so this is already about a million times  smaller  than the millimeter size of the coffee  bean that we started from  here we encounter viruses that are so  small that they can easily penetrate  into our cells and start affecting their  function  by injecting their own genetic material  into the machinery of the cell  essentially they're hijacking our own  molecular factories  to produce copies of the virus and in  this sense the computer analogy is a  pretty good one  the virus doesn't have any hardware to  produce copies of itself  its only way to spread is to infect a  host cell  and use its hardware to make copies of  itself and finally if we zoom  in beyond the scale of the smallest  viruses  we encounter the domain of proteins  proteins are essentially small nano  machines that perform  very specific functions inside of living  organisms  here for example you can see depiction  of what's called a ribosome  it's a complex molecular machine found  in all living cells  that essentially translates the genetic  code written in our dna  and uses it to produce new proteins  but before we take a closer look at how  exactly all of this works let's first  finish our biological zoom all the way  to the bottom  here's another protein called hemoglobin  and its function is very simply to  capture oxygen molecules in our lungs  and transport them across the body  releasing them  inside our cells where they are needed  for breaking down the sugars  in the mitochondria that we just saw and  then finally if we dive below the  nanometer scale  we arrive at the very smallest  biological compounds  like glucose and adenine that are made  up of just a few individual atoms  so the main thing to remember here is  that all of biology is a tremendously  complex hierarchy of  interacting systems each of which is  following the laws of nature  but gaining complexity as we move up the  scale ladder  in theory with an infinitely powerful  computer  you could solve all open questions about  biology  by simply simulating a living system  down to the atom level  so now that we have an idea of the  various components  that play a role in this molecular dance  let's take a look at some of the most  fundamental processes in this play of  life  specifically we'll start by taking a  look at how  exactly the genetic code embedded in our  dna  contains the executive program on which  all of these processes run so buckle  your seat belt as we dive into the most  fundamental process that powers life on  earth  so deep inside the nucleus of every cell  in our body we find  the chromosomes they're essentially huge  storage containers  for our genetic material humans actually  have 46 of these chromosomes which  together contain what is called the  human genome or in other words  the molecular blueprint that contains  all the information that you need  to build a human body now the genetic  material  inside of those chromosomes is tightly  wrapped around proteins called  histones to keep things nice and tidy if  it weren't for those histones  the long dna strand would become a  completely  tangled mess now what happens when our  cell  needs to build new machinery is that a  specific section of this dna  called a gene will be read for  instructions on how to produce  a specific protein the whole process  starts with an already existing protein  called  rna polymerase that latches onto the dna  and unwinds the double helix into a flat  section of genetic code using its  complex machinery it will then start to  copy the instructions encoded in the dna  sequence  in a process called transcription now in  this phase  each letter of the gene is faithfully  copied over into a complementary chain  of base pairs  that essentially contains the same  information as the gene itself  the result of this transcription is a  chain of messenger  rna which contains the relevant part of  the genetic code  that's needed to create a specific  protein  and once this is done the messenger rna  moves  out of the nucleus and travels into the  cytoplasm of the cell  there it encounters another type of  proteins called  ribosomes which are like the protein  factories of our cells  so where the dna polymerase are the guys  that figure out exactly  how to build proteins the ribosomes  actually get the job done  so the ribosome binds to this messenger  rna and it starts  reading its instructions in order to  produce a new protein  now the fundamental building blocks of  proteins  are what are called amino acids these  are  small molecular compounds that come in  unique flavors  and they consist of only about 10 to 20  atoms so  these things are really really small in  fact there are exactly 20 different  types of amino acids in ordinary biology  which you can think of as the essential  lego pieces  that biology uses to make stuff it's  like  the fundamental alphabet of life freely  floating around in the cytoplasm of the  cell every one of these amino acids  is connected to a piece of transfer rna  which contains a unique identifier at  the bottom that  perfectly matches a specific  three-letter sequence  in the genetic code the ribosome reads  the messenger rna  three bases at a time and as each  triplet is read  the connected transfer rna delivers  the corresponding lego block to a  growing  chain of amino acids that exits the  ribosome at the top  and after the last amino acid has been  added the purposefully ordered chain  is then released into the cytoplasm of  the cell's interior  which is essentially full of water  molecules  there it is subject to a complex  interaction of physical forces both  among the amino acids in the chain  as well as with the surrounding water  molecules and this  finally causes the entire chain to fold  in on itself  and to form a complex but unique  three-dimensional structure  which is called a protein and that's why  this  entire process is called translation  because through this beautifully complex  mechanism  the molecular engine of life is able to  translate  a piece of genetic code into an  operational protein  that can now go and take up its own  proper function  somewhere else in the body all right so  we've just seen  that the ordering of the dna letters in  the gene that we start from  controls the ordering of the amino acid  sequence in the protein chain  and after that the laws of physics cause  that chain to  fold up into the lowest energy state  that it can reach  in fact this is exactly what people mean  when they talk about protein  folding and it's exactly this task that  deepmind's alpha fold model addresses  essentially  deepmind trained a carefully designed  neural network architecture  to predict the three-dimensional shape  of a protein  knowing only its amino acid sequence  they did this using a very large data  set of known protein structures which is  called  the protein data bank or pdb and  given that this approach recently  started to work amazingly well  it's actually opening up an incredibly  exciting  area of ai assisted protein design which  is the space that i'm working in right  now  but we will talk a lot more about that  in future videos in fact  once deepind eventually releases their  academic alpha phil2 paper  hopefully with code and models included  i intend to make an  entire video dedicated to protein  folding  all right fair enough so a protein has a  unique  shape that's entirely defined by its  amino acid sequence  which is coded up in our dna but why  should that have  anything to do with a protein's function  you might ask  well a good intuitive way to think about  this is to imagine proteins as small  three-dimensional puzzle pieces  that fit into certain pockets but don't  fit in most  others additionally many of these  proteins are not  really fixed rigid structures but they  can actually  change shape depending on what they're  chemically bound  to to give a concrete example of this  let's take a look  at a very specific pair of two proteins  that absolutely blew my mind when i  first learned about them  so i'm talking about the motor protein  pair consisting of  dynein and kinesin and the main function  of these motor proteins  is to move stuff around inside of the  cell  so it turns out that pretty much every  cell in your body  contains what are called microtubules  which are essentially long cables that  are stretched between  opposite sides of the cell barrier now  these microtubules they function as  rigid structural highways that the  dynein and kinesin proteins  interact with in order to move stuff  around  in your cells and the way this works is  that kinesin for example consists of  two identical motor heads at one end  and a long and flexible organelle at the  other that attaches to the cargo  that's being moved around now the shape  of the motor heads  is such that when it comes into contact  with a microtubule  it establishes a strong chemical bond  which releases a small adp molecule  from the now attached head and almost  instantaneously then this  empty binding site is filled up by an  atp  molecule which triggers a shape change  of the motor head  causing the other leg to be thrown  forward and  also bind to the microtubule and when  this happens  it triggers a chemical reaction in the  first head  which causes it to let go of the  microtubule  and the process loops back from the  beginning  essentially causing the kinesin motor to  start  walking along the microtubule track  with its two alternating legs and  pulling along anything that's attached  to it i mean  is it isn't that just absolutely  incredible  and these proteins actually do a lot  more than just  carry around stuff within cells in fact  similar kinds of proteins are  responsible for any kind of  muscle contraction in your body i mean  just think about this for a second every  time that you move a muscle in your body  millions of these tiny molecular engines  purposefully  designed by the genetic code in our dna  instantly  react to an electrical signal coming  from your brain  and they start changing shape and  physically  interacting with other proteins causing  your  muscle fibers to contract in your body  to move  also interesting is that relative to  their size  these motor proteins move about as fast  as an  actual car on a highway but they are  four  times more efficient than your car in  converting chemical energy  into motion so you see i mean we still  have a lot left to learn from mother  nature  so what you should remember is that the  driving mechanism of protein function  is the chemical interactions that they  have with other substrates in the body  and the way that these interactions  happen is  largely controlled by their shape so to  give a second example of why  shape is so important in the protein  world let's take a look  at the biggest biological nemesis of  recent times  the coronavirus so the coronavirus  is a single encased cell surrounded on  the outside  with the so-called spike protein which  gives the virus its crown-like look  and hence its name now because of its  shape  this spike protein has a particular  affinity  to binding with a specific protein in  the human body  which is the ace2 receptor on the  surface of human lung cells  so what happens is that the virus is  just roaming around in the body  until it encounters some lung cells  where the spike  protein then binds with this h2 receptor  this then triggers a protein-powered  shape  change whereby the viral rna enters the  human cell  and it hijacks our own cellular  machinery  to start making copies of the virus  fortunately  the protein mechanism that makes viruses  so dangerous  is also their weakness think about the  capsid on the outside  as a three-dimensional fingerprint that  uniquely identifies each type of virus  in fact this is exactly how our body is  able to mount a defense against the  virus attack  by purposefully manufacturing antibodies  that bind to the capsid of a specific  virus  our adaptive immune system can create a  highly targeted defense  and if you're lucky your immune system  will remember how to make those specific  antibodies for many years to come  which is precisely what we call being  immune this  is also how a lot of vaccines work you  simply  manufacture some of those capsid  proteins in a lab  and you inject them into the patient  without an actual virus being present  the immune system will detect this  strange compound and it will start  creating specific antibodies that binds  to that protein signature  so that when the virus actually comes  around our body can mount a highly  specialized attack almost  instantaneously  so i think by now it's becoming clear  why ai models like deepmind's alpha fold  have such an  incredible impact on areas like drug  development and vaccine design  because if we can use ai to predict the  structures of most important proteins in  biology  and model their interactions well this  gives us  amazing new insights to how they  function looking  at the future then many people agree  that this is the beginning of an  entirely  new era in computational biology  as ai continues to get better at  modeling the complexity  of the real world it will open up new  opportunities to cure  incurable diseases treat cancer and will  likely become a core component  of any biomedical design cycle there is  also tremendous potential  in a field called bioengineering that  tries to harness the efficient molecular  mechanisms that nature discovered  through evolution motor proteins for  example are four  times as efficient in converting  chemical energy into motion  compared to a regular gasoline engine so  what if we could engineer an engine  based on motor proteins  and all it would take to run was a  little bit of sugar  or what if we could use living cells to  grow  new organic materials with exceptional  properties like we see in bamboo or  tooth enamel there's even people working  on designing new enzymes that can  actually break up  environmental waste materials like hard  plastics  that are not traditionally biodegradable  needless to say that with the  ever-growing amount of available data  and the sheer complexity  of the processes involved machine  learning is positioned  as a very powerful tool for many of  these scientific disciplines  so in the next few videos we'll dive  into some of the specifics of how to  apply  deep learning to various areas of  computational biology  like protein structure prediction  protein design property prediction  and much more so whether you look at it  from a purely scientific perspective or  you're more of an instinctive type of  person  life is undoubtedly the most astounding  miracle in the universe  and we are smack in the middle of it so  the next time you're getting some corona  blues  just look at your own hand and  contemplate you know the astounding  complexity  of molecular machinery that is hard at  work  to keep you very much alive all right  and before ending this video i wanted to  quickly mention that  i'm trying out a new platform called  pensite where i'm available for  one-on-one video calls in case you have  questions about specific projects  that you're working on or any kind of  machine learning question in general  link is in the description hope to see  you there  you

